_Remark 5.7_.: The velocity vector \(\mathbf{u}\) has a vector potential that satisfies (5.39) only if \(\Omega\) is simply-connected. However, it is worth mentioning that Problem (5.44) is well-posed even when \(\Omega\) is multiply-connected. In fact, we can easily prove the following equivalence of norms.

**Lemma 5.2**.: _In addition to the hypotheses of Theorem 5.1, assume that either \(\Gamma\) is \(\mathcal{C}^{1,\,1}\) or that \(\Omega\) is simply-connected. Then the mapping \(\psi\to\|\,A\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}\) is a norm on \(\Psi\) equivalent to \(\|\psi\|\)._

Proof.: First, observe that \(\|\psi\|\) is equivalent on \(\Psi\) to:

\[\{\,\|\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2}+\|\operatorname{div}\psi\|_{1,\,\Omega}^{2}+| \operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi|_{1,\,\Omega}^{2}\}^{1/2}\]

by virtue of Theorem 1.1. Next, remark that (5.41) is simply the orthogonal decomposition of the vector \(\Delta\mathbf{0}\). This orthogonality yields:

\[\|\,\Delta\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2}=\|\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\operatorname {\mathbf{curl}}\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2}+|\operatorname{div}\psi|_{1,\,\Omega} ^{2}.\]

But since \(\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi\in H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)^{3}\), we infer from Remark 2.7 that

\[|\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi|_{1,\,\Omega}\cong\|\operatorname{\mathbf{ curl}}\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}.\]

Besides that, the fact that \(\int_{\Omega}\operatorname{div}\psi\,dx=0\) implies that:

\[\|\operatorname{div}\psi\|_{1,\,\Omega}\cong|\operatorname{div}\psi|_{1,\, \Omega}.\]

Indeed, a straightforward application of Theorem 2.1,3\({}^{\circ}\)) yields:

\[\|\,v\|_{1,\,\Omega}\cong\left(|v|_{1,\,\Omega}^{2}+\left|\int_{\Omega}v\,dx \right|^{2}\right)^{1/2}\quad\forall v\in H^{1}(\Omega).\]

Hence it remains to establish that

(5.45) \[\|\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}\leqslant C\{\,\|\operatorname{div}\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}^ {2}+\|\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2}\}^{1/2}\quad\quad \forall\psi\in\Psi.\]

When \(\Omega\) is simply-connected, this is proved by Lemma 3.4. Indeed, even if \(\Gamma\) has several components, its argument shows that:

\[\|\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}\leqslant C\,\|\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi\|_{0, \,\Omega}\quad\quad\forall\psi\in\Psi\quad\text{with}\quad\operatorname{div} \psi=0.\]

And if \(\operatorname{div}\psi\neq 0\), we can always obtain a divergence-free function in \(\Psi\) by considering the difference \(\psi\,-\,\mathbf{w}\) where \(\mathbf{w}\in H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)^{3}\) satisfies (cf. Corollary 2.4):

\[\operatorname{div}\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{div}\psi,\quad\quad|\mathbf{w}|_{1, \,\Omega}\leqslant C\,\|\operatorname{div}\psi\|_{0,\,\Omega}.\]

When \(\Gamma\) is \(\mathcal{C}^{1,\,1}\), (5.45) is an easy consequence of Theorems 3.7, 2.1 and Remark 3.9. 

_Remark 5.8_.: It can also be proved (cf. Dominguez [24]) that when \(\Omega\) is multiply-connected, the solution of Problem (5.44) is not the potential of the original Stokes problem.
